9. Palindrome Number

# Intuition
The palindrome program checks if the given number is palindrome or not. A palindrome is simply a number which is same when read left to right or right to left. There was a more definitive way of using two pointers. But this approach is more readable and easy to understand.

# Approach
First we define three variables. One for holding the value of the given number (later used for comparison) because the given number will be mutated in the intermediate steps. Then 'lastNumber' holds the value of the last digit to append while we reverse the number; the 'reverse' variable does that job. Then we check if the values are non negative, we use the modulo operator to extract the last digit, multiply by it's place (tens, hundreds, etc.) and then add the lastDigit and append it to the reverse variable untill all the digits are exhausted. Then we reduce the 'x' by 10 each step so that the lastNumber gets a new value everytime.

# Complexity
- Time complexity:
O(log n) ---> The time complexity grows by the increase in the digits and not the actual number itself

- Space complexity:
O(1) ---> No new array or data structure similar to the size of input is being created.

# Code
```java []
class Solution {
    public boolean isPalindrome(int x) {
        int reverse = 0;
        int lastNumber = 0;
        int original = x;

        if (x < 0) {
            return false;
        }

        while (x > 0) {
            lastNumber = x % 10;
            reverse = reverse * 10 + lastNumber;
            x /= 10;
        }
    return reverse == original;
    }
}
```

